<div xmlns:perseus="http://www.perseus.org/meta/perseus.rdfs#" xmlns:dctype="http://purl.org/dc/dcmitype/" xmlns:tufts="http://www.tufts.edu/" xmlns:alph="http://alpheios.net/namespaces/tei" class="alpheios-lex-text_container en">
    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n17332" body-key=""><b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">extento</span></b> , āre, v.
			freq. a.
		id., <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>I.</b><i>to stretch out</i>, <i>extend</i> (ante- and
			post-class.). </div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>I.</b>
			<b>Lit.</b>: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">nervos,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Lucr.
					3, 490</span></i>
			: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">humeros angustos,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Amm. 22, 14</span></i>
			.—</div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>II.</b>
			<b>Trop.</b>: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">vires alieno ostio,</span>”
				i. e. <i>to exert</i>, <i>try</i>,
				 <i><span class="en">Plaut.
					Bacch. 4, 2, 3</span></i>
			: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">quid tu venisti huc te extentatum?</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">id.
					Most. 3, 1, 66</span></i>
			, v. Ritschl ad h. l.</div>
